Nginx default config listens 80. We'll replace default with our config. Keep a backup? It's a fresh container; fine. Write /etc/nginx/sites-available/win311 and symlink default to it (remove existing default symlink). Or overwrite /etc/nginx/sites-available/default. Simpler: write /etc/nginx/sites-available/win311, remove sites-enabled/default, symlink win311.

Config:
server {
  listen 80 default_server;
  listen [::]:80 default_server;
  server_name _;
  root /usr/share/novnc;
  index index.html;
  location = / { return 302 /vnc.html?autoconnect=true&path=websockify&resize=scale; }
  location / { try_files $uri $uri/ =404; }
  location /websockify {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:6080;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
    proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_read_timeout 3600s;
    proxy_send_timeout 3600s;
  }
}

Could also create index.html that embeds noVNC in an iframe for a nicer web interface. A redirect to vnc.html is fine.

Note: noVNC vnc.html with `autoconnect=true` and `path=websockify`; host defaults to current, port defaults to 80? noVNC uses `window.location.hostname` and `port` from URL or 80/443. It should connect to ws://host/websockify. Good.

Start websockify: `/usr/bin/websockify 127.0.0.1:6080 127.0.0.1:5901` with nohup. It runs Python, log to /var/log/websockify.log. Use `--web /usr/share/novnc`? We don't need web; nginx serves. Running without --web is fine. To ensure it can handle WebSocket proxy, yes.

Test nginx -t, start/reload. Then curl (wget) http://127.0.0.1/ and check 302 or 200. Use Python urllib (follow redirects). Also test WebSocket? Could use python websocket client? noVNC session check difficult. We can verify TCP ports listen and HTTP page served.

